WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:04.640
Every now and then, Wolfe and Fritz sniff
the scent of a one-of-a-kind antique and chase

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.600
it to a place that's dark, strange, or even
dangerous.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.560
Here are some times when American Pickers
found weirdness along with the treasures.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.180
When Captain Kirk from Star Trek makes an
order, you take it.

00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:19.540
He'd contacted the Antique Archaeology and
spoke with Danielle Colby, who passed along

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:24.050
the message to Mike Wolfe and Frank Fritz
that William Shatner wanted, nay needed, an

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.720
antique millstone for his garden.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.500
Sure enough, because they're the best in the
business, the Pickers found a millstone, a

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.420
gigantic tool used to process grain.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.530
Hardly a jaded Hollywood big shot, Shatner
made the surreal moment even more delicious

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.020
with some breathless declarations

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.600
"This is the signature piece.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:40.800
The millstone is genius."

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.960
In a second-season episode, the Pickers went
deep into Kentucky, to the home of an older

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.680
couple named Don and Nannette.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:52.200
They had a barn where they'd tossed all kinds
of junk, from branded signs to old TV sets.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.860
As Wolfe looked around, he noticed a curtain
that would otherwise have blended into the

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:56.360
wall.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.480
Apparently not worried about deadly spiders
or ghosts, he boldly just stuck his head through

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:05.110
the opening and came out wearing a very old,
oversized, grotesque mask of screen comedian

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:05.880
Stan Laurel.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.940
But where there's Laurel, there's longtime
comedy partner Oliver Hardy.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.860
Sure enough, there was another, even creepier
mask that Fritz put on his head.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.580
The masks were crumbling and probably haunted,
but the pair bought them anyway.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.120
The Pickers have paid multiple visits to the
home of Ron, a.k.a.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:20.780
"The Mole Man."

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:25.080
Beneath a typical-looking American Pickers
rural property, with several outbuildings

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:28.180
filled with intriguing prospects, is Ron's
actual home.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.040
It's more than 20 feet beneath, in fact.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.070
In the 2013 episode, Wolfe and Fritz arrive
to Ron's home unannounced and attempt to navigate

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:40.040
the seemingly endless, cavernous, maze-like
tunnels that Ron's been developing since 1965.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:44.860
The guys squeeze through narrow tunnels, walking
on narrow planks past wood, cinder blocks,

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.800
and junk until they get to a dead end and
have to try another route.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.780
“This place is like Indiana Jones meets
Sanford and Son.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.990
The narrow path, you know, all that stuff.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:53.960
I'm jivin' on this."

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.880
Toward the end they unwisely hurry through,
creeped out as they are, until they reach

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:02.180
Ron's door, found beyond a single piece of
wood placed over a 20-foot-deep pit.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.740
On a picking swing through New England in
2018, Wolfe and Fritz visited a lushly wooded

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.040
property in Chesterfield, Massachusetts.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:13.810
The owner took them to a remote corner of
his land and showed them a thoroughly rusted

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:18.230
vehicle decorated with a long-nosed cartoon
character in a striped shirt and the word

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:18.940
"Aerosmith."

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.220
Could this derelict van actually have belonged
to the band Aerosmith?

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:23.660
They were local to Boston.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.780
Wolfe managed to make contact with an early
member of Aerosmith who confirmed that yes,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.080
this was the band's touring van.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.500
Upon looking through the van, the Pickers
found Persian rugs, old Budweiser cans, a

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.420
Three Dog Night sticker, and two of the filthiest
mattresses that ever existed.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:43.020
Homer Tate was an American original; a pioneer
in the uniquely American art form of the "roadside

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:43.520
attraction."

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.540
He specialized in making hideous, macabre
"artifacts" out of papier-mâché

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.400
mud, and real animal bones.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:54.260
Then he advertised them — as real finds,
not artistic creations — in the 1940s and

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.300
'50s, getting people to stop and take a gander
for a small fee.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.750
Tate is probably best known for "The Thing,"
an Arizona roadside oddity purporting to be

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:03.220
a mummified mother and child.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:07.230
He eventually opened Tate's Curiosity Shop
to display his wares, and it's full of the

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.420
kind of stuff the American Pickers love to
find.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.120
Indeed, Wolfe and Fritz came face to face
with "Wolf Boy," a Homer Tate original that

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.870
looks like a combination of E.T. and a turtle,
if either creature had fangs.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:22.680
The story is that it's one of 26 mummies discovered
in a cave in Peru, but it's merely an eerie

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.180
fake.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:27.740
No matter what a viewer's opinion on taxidermy
may be, they'd have to admit that a giant

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:31.860
building in rural New England in which every
square inch is occupied by a dead, stuffed

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.340
animal is pretty weird.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.700
Wolfe and Fritz visited that very place in
a 2012 American Pickers episode, and grew

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:42.420
transfixed with two well preserved elephant
heads, one partially real, the other entirely

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.100
artificial.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.620
Wolfe decided he simply had to have the mostly
real one, and paid an astounding $9,500 for

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:49.120
it.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.120
With so much capital tied up in the elephant
head, the Pickers needed to unload it quickly

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.700
as possible, and for as much money as possible.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.020
They tasked Danielle back at the shop with
finding a buyer.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.360
"When I sent the boys to pick taxidermy stuff,
I thought maybe they'd come back with a deer

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.380
head, some antlers, squirrel.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.480
No, they come back with an elephant head.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:07.860
Who buys an elephant head?"

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:12.500
Astonishingly, she located one in rock star
Jack White, a guy who loves pachyderms so

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:16.340
much he named the White Stripes' classic 2003
album Elephant.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:20.600
White tried to talk down the price, but the
Pickers, having sunk such a huge sum of cash

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.780
into their find, didn't have a lot of wiggle
room.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.020
Eventually, White proposed a deal: He'd get
the elephant head at their price if he threw

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:31.630
in some American Pickers-worthy bits of old-school
Americana, specifically a black-and-white

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:33.260
photobooth and a jukebox.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.880
The Pickers took the deal, then had to shell
out an extra $1,000 to ship the elephant head.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:42.680
Rockford, Illinois is famously the home of
the great American rock band Cheap Trick,

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.870
and Fritz — a big fan — was extremely
excited to see Rick's Picks, a rock n' roll

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:51.910
museum curated by and featuring the assorted
musical memorabilia of Cheap Trick guitarist

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:52.740
Rick Nielsen.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:56.780
Known for his boisterous stage personality,
Nielsen's huge collection wowed Fritz and

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:57.360
Wolfe.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:02.060
It included a number of the musician's famous
novelty guitars, including a check-patterned

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:04.140
instrument and one with five necks.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:08.120
Nielsen invited the Pickers to his warehouse
to see even more of his stuff, and the guys

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:13.620
dropped about $2,500 on some albums, a poster,
T-shirts, and traveling cases.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.460
But they really wanted to take home one of
Nielsen's guitars.

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:19.760
They haggled for a while, but Nielsen just
wouldn't surrender his piece.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:23.460
He finally let Fritz and Wolfe have it for
free, on the condition that they keep it on

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.340
display at Antique Archaeology — they can't
sell it.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.440
"Keep it in Nashville so I can play it on
Saturday or Sunday, or--"

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:30.760
"When you comin' in?"

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.180
"You can play it whenever you want!"

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:33.580
"You guys take it down there."

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:34.700
"Are you serious?

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:35.200
I'm serious."

